        "path": "PLAN.md — “Источник данных” / `fetch_cheapest`",
        "evidence": "The plan rejects `aviasales/v3/prices_for_dates` as nonexistent and chooses `/v2/prices/latest`. Current official Travelpayouts Data API documentation lists `/aviasales/v3/prices_for_dates`, explicitly recommends it instead of `/v2/prices/latest`, limits results to tickets found in the last 48 hours, and returns a route/date-specific `link`. The selected cached endpoint therefore conflicts with the claimed research and unnecessarily cannot provide the specified ticket-search link.",
        "fix": "Use `/aviasales/v3/prices_for_dates` with each route’s departure/return constraints and consume its returned link; revise the documented API rationale and response parsing accordingly."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-002",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `config.py` ROUTES and `fetch_cheapest` query parameters",
        "evidence": "A route has `one_way` and `currency`, but the specified `/v2/prices/latest` request includes only origin, destination, period_type, beginning_of_period, sorting, and show_to_affiliates. Thus a configured round-trip route can be fetched as the endpoint default (one-way), and a configured non-default currency can silently be stored and alerted in the API default currency. Both make the route configuration and price comparison incorrect.",
        "fix": "Specify and implement propagation of `one_way` and `currency` for every request, validate accepted values, and include both in the stable route identity so histories of different trip types/currencies never mix."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-003",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `check_route`, `record_price`, and alert behavior",
        "evidence": "No alert state, uniqueness key, cooldown, or post-send persistence is planned. A low cached fare returned unchanged by the API on consecutive 20-second polls remains below the median and will produce a Telegram alert every cycle, potentially indefinitely. The shared SQLite `prices` table only stores observations, so process restart also cannot suppress already-alerted fares.",
        "fix": "Add durable alert state keyed by route configuration plus fare/date/link (or a defined cooldown), write it transactionally only after a successful send, and avoid repeated observations/alerts for an unchanged API record."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-004",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — acceptance criterion 3 and Verification step 3",
        "evidence": "Setting `ANOMALY_THRESHOLD=0.95` does not guarantee an alert within one poll: the criterion itself requires at least `MIN_HISTORY_SAMPLES` prior observations, and an unchanged cached cheapest price equals its rolling median rather than being below 95% of it. The manual verification text later says to wait five cycles, contradicting the one-cycle acceptance criterion, and still does not ensure a lower next price.",
        "fix": "Replace this with a deterministic fixture/mocked response test that seeds history and supplies a lower price; describe the real-token check as observational rather than a guaranteed acceptance test."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-005",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `main()` polling all routes",
        "evidence": "The plan sets a 20-second interval but gives no route-count limit, pacing, or handling policy for HTTP 429. `/v2/prices/latest` is limited to 300 requests/minute; more than 100 routes produces over 300 requests/minute before retries. Retrying 429 once per cycle can keep the service rate-limited and leave routes unobserved.",
        "fix": "Enforce a safe maximum configured route count or schedule requests with rate limiting; treat 429/Retry-After as a per-endpoint backoff and log skipped/deferred routes."
      },
